              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_20_table_2.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>General repeating</td><td>• Let x = the decimal number. • Multiply by 10m where m = the number of non-repeating digits, then multiply by 10n where n = the number of repeating digits. • Subtract from the previous equation, and solve for x.</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.245,
            0.558,
            0.268
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.6.2 The Magic of Cyclic Numbers"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.28,
            0.886,
            0.369
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When we calculate the decimal for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 7 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " we get 0.142857142857 … or 0.142857. The repeating block, 142857, is one of mathematics’ most fascinating gem —  a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cyclic number",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Watch what happens when we multiply it by the digits 1 through 6:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.373,
            0.63,
            0.395
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_20_equation_5.jpg",
          "content": "1 4 2 8 5 7 \\times 1 = 1 4 2 8 5 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.399,
            0.629,
            0.422
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_20_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "142857 \\times 2 = 285714"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.426,
            0.627,
            0.447
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_20_equation_7.jpg",
          "content": "142857 \\times 3 = 428571"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.452,
            0.629,
            0.474
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_20_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "142857 \\times 4 = 571428"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.478,
            0.629,
            0.5
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_20_equation_9.jpg",
          "content": "142857 \\times 5 = 714285"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.506,
            0.63,
            0.527
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_20_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "1 4 2 8 5 7 \\times 6 = 8 5 7 1 4 2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.537,
            0.885,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The same digits simply shift in a cyclic circle! This beautiful internal structure is a hallmark of the rational number "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 7 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.614,
            0.679,
            0.637
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.6.3 Irrational Decimals: Chaos and Infinity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.643,
            0.885,
            0.703
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Irrational numbers, however, possess decimal expansions that never end and never repeat. There is no cyclic block, no pattern that loops forever. Examples include:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.35,
            0.711,
            0.694,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2 = 1.4142135623730950488 …"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.742,
            0.693,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "π = 3.1415926535897932384 …"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.797,
            0.622,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 3.5"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.844,
            0.886,
            0.921
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Without performing long division, determine which of the following rational numbers will have terminating decimals and which will be repeating: "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 7 } { 2 0 } , \\frac { 4 } { 1 5 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 3 } { 2 5 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Then check your answers"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
